      "figure": "Fig. 2",
      "caption": "FIG. 2. Clock laser frequency-noise stabilization. (a) A simplified schematic of the stabilization chain from the reference cavities to the atoms. Servo feedback is indicated by blue arrows, and laser light is depicted as red arrows. A 1542-nm laser is PDH locked to the Si3 cavity with a bandwidth of 100 kHz, which provides the low-frequency stability (f ≲ 100 Hz) of the entire system. A self-referenced frequency comb is phase locked to the Si3-cavity-stabilized 1542-nm laser with a bandwidth of 600 kHz. High-frequency noise (f ≳ 1 kHz) is reduced by PDH locking a 698-nm ECDL to a ULE cavity with 1-MHz bandwidth (blue-shaded region). The stability transfer at low Fourier frequency is achieved by phase locking the ULE-cavity stabilized light to the Si3-cavity-stabilized comb via actuating on an AOM with a bandwidth of 500 Hz (purple-shaded region). This setup represents a low-power (approximately 10 mW) spectrally tailored laser with optimal noise performance. Finally, the light delivered to the atoms is provided by a high-power (≳1 W) laser phase locked to the stabilized 698-nm light with a bandwidth of 400 kHz (green-shaded region).",
