      "authors": "Kai Sun et al.",
      "figure": "Fig. 2",
      "caption": "Figure 2. DKA peer-review process An example review process using two papers $( P _ { 1 }$ and $P _ { 2 }$ ) in an academic conference setting. The review process operates through four sequential stages. (1) Settingup stage: scholars who would like to participate in the conference create their conference accounts and purchase tokens from the community coinbase as proof of membership. In this example, scholars $( S _ { 1 }$ S2; S3; S4) initialize accunts with token balances ranging from 5 to 20 tokens. (2) Submission stage: scholars submit papers to the conference by creating paper accounts and transferring certain amounts of conference tokens $Q _ { s } \\in R ^ { + }$ as a submission fee, demonstrated by $S _ { 1 }$ submitting $P _ { 1 }$ $\\begin{array} { r l } { { \\sf 2 } _ { 1 } } & { { } ( B a I ( S _ { 1 } ) = } \\end{array}$ $^ { 1 5 \\to 1 0 ) }$ and $S _ { 4 }$ submitting $P _ { 2 }$ $( B a I ( S _ { 4 } ) ~ = ~ 2 0 { } 1 5 )$ ). (3) Review stage: scholars browse submissions and select papers for review. A reviewer can either submit a constructive review on a paper by paying ${ \\cal Q } _ { + } \\in R ^ { + }$ tokens to the paper’s account or submit a critical review to earn $Q _ { - } \\in R ^ { + }$ tokens from the paper’s account. and (4) Post-review stage: final token allocation occurs where remaining balances are withdrawn to authors. This figure provides a concrete example, which demonstrates token conservation and quantitative feedback through reviewer interactions, with paper success measured by final token accumulation.",
