      "caption": "Fig 2\n\nAbomasal Th2 immune response to H. contortus infection.\n\nFollowing the arrival of H. contortus larvae to the abomasum, the activation of innate immune cells responds to produce cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 to support the development of the T-helper 2 type (Th2) polarization. Antigen-presenting cells such as dendritic cells leave the abomasum and travel to local lymph nodes such as the abomasal lymph nodes to prime naïve CD4+ T cells for subsequent activation and differentiation. The naïve CD4+ T cells differentiate into Th2 cells where they travel to the site of infection to amplify cellular responses through the production of Th2-like cytokines and to activate B cell class switching and IgE and IgA production through IL-4 production, facilitating worm expulsion. Created with BioRender.com/l2pretl.",
