      "id": "2736fb2e",
      "diagram": "diagram/2736fb2e.png",
      "class": "Mechanism Diagram",
      "domain": "1",
      "title": "Metabolism meets immunity: TCA-cycle activation protects the vulnerable newborn",
      "authors": "Tineke Vanderhaeghen; Claude Libert; Jolien Vandewalle",
      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1\n\nRedirecting energy metabolism from glycolysis to the TCA cycle via parenteral nutrition improves neonatal sepsis outcome.\n\nIn preterm infants, glucose-rich parenteral nutrition is commonly administered during the first weeks of life. However, this practice skews metabolism to glycolysis instead of oxidative phosphorylation (OxPhos). Increased reliance on glycolysis is associated with acidosis, hyperglycaemia, excessive inflammation, organ injury, and increased susceptibility to infection, thereby aggravating sepsis severity. Replacing glucose with an isocaloric amount of galactose in a preterm pig neonatal sepsis model induces a slower conversion of galactose into glucose-6-phosphate, thereby reducing systemic glycolytic flux while increasing dependence on TCA cycle activity and mitochondrial OxPhos. This metabolic rewiring attenuated the pro-inflammatory activity of immune cells without compromising energy homeostasis or bacterial clearance. Moreover, blood glucose and pH levels remained stable, contributing to reduced lethality. Similarly, supplementation of glucogenic amino acids (GAAs; glutamate, valine, aspartate, and asparagine) together with restricted glucose infusion enhanced TCA cycle activity, as these amino acids enter the cycle either directly or through intermediary metabolites. This intervention promoted normoglycemia, reduced bacterial burden (disease resistance), and preserved blood pH despite comparable bacterial loads (disease tolerance), ultimately providing complete protection against infection-induced mortality in piglets. Created in BioRender. https://BioRender.com/7g8l5pw.",
      "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s44321-026-00462-0",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
      "condition": "Specific BioRender page explicitly licenses this Completed Graphic under CC BY 4.0; individual BioRender icons may not be extracted and redistributed separately. Article permissions cover the saved author text under CC BY 4.0. Selected excerpts and captions contain no identified credit requiring separate permission. Other article images are omitted. Image bytes unchanged; complete BioRender public PNG, no cropping or icon extraction. Original-language article paragraphs and linked captions converted from JATS XML to Markdown; styling removed, no OCR, translation or paraphrase. Graphic: Figure 1: Redirecting energy metabolism from glycolysis to TCA cycle via parenteral nutrition improves neonatal sepsis outcome. Creator: Jolien Vandewalle. Provider: BioRender. Asset source: https://biorender.com/7g8l5pw. License evidence: https://app.biorender.com/citation/6a0f2aca3184381ba7379ba1. “Figure 1: Redirecting energy metabolism from glycolysis to TCA cycle via parenteral nutrition improves neonatal sepsis outcome” created in BioRender. Vandewalle, J. (https://BioRender.com/7g8l5pw) is licensed under CC BY 4.0. Article caption explicitly links to the complete BioRender asset; may correspond to a panel or construction sheet rather than the entire published figure.",
