      "figure": "Fig. 4",
      "caption": "Fig. 4\n\nKATP channel is constitutively active in SVD ACT pericytes.\n\na, Workflow showing microvascular integrity-maintained perforated patch. b, Experimental protocol. c, Representative traces of basal (top) and glibenclamide-sensitive (bottom) currents. d, Inward base currents for WT and SVD ACT pericytes measured at −70 mV. *P = 0.0155 via two-sided Mann–Whitney U-test (n = 6, 8 mice). e, Glibenclamide-sensitive inward currents for WT and SVD measured at −70 mV, using 10 μM glibenclamide. *P = 0.0255 via two-sided, unpaired Student’s t-test (n = 6 mice). f, CCD brightfield image from IonOptix software showing edge detection of capillary lumina and pericytes. g, Pharmacological representation of KATP channel agonist and antagonist. h, Representative traces of WT and SVD capillary luminal diameter over time and with KATP pharmacological treatment. i, Pinacidil-induced change in diameter, shown as a percentage change from baseline. Both 1 μM and 10 μM concentrations are reported. *P = 0.0192 and **P = 0.0062 via two-sided ANOVA, post-hoc Dunn’s multiple-comparison test (n = 6 and 8 mice). j, Glibenclamide-induced change in diameter (percentage change from baseline), using 10 μM glibenclamide. **P = 0.017 via two-sided, unpaired Student’s t-test (n = 8 mice). k, Representative traces of glucose curve for WT and SVD. l, Summary data of glibenclamide-induced constriction in the presence of glucose. *P = 0.0189, 0.0161, 0.0369; **P = 0.0027; ***P = 0.0045; and ****P < 0.0001 per repeated-measure ANOVA with post-hoc Bonferroni’s correction (n = 4 mice). Glib., glibenclamide; pin., pinacidil. Illustration in a created in BioRender; Jeffrey, D. https://biorender.com/cyv44hn (2026).\n\nSource data",
