      "caption": "Fig. 5\n\nSVD ACT zone displays decreased sensitivity to pinacidil and increased vasoconstriction to glibenclamide compared to WT.\n\na, Experimental workflow as acute cranial window and pharmacological representation of KATP channel agonist or antagonist. b,c, Superior view of representative cranial windows from both WT (b) and SVD (c) mice expressing SMMHC-GCaMP6f with TRITC-dextran lumen diameter fill. Zoomed-in sections show filament renderings and segment diameter heatmaps under basal 10 μΜ pinacidil treatment and 10 μM glibenclamide treatment. The dashed box indicates a parenchymal arteriole and the corresponding ACT zone, where the mean diameter was taken for each segment. A heatmap of the mean segment diameter (μm) is displayed with percentage change in diameter shown for each treatment. b, Schematic of in vivo acute cranial window and Imaris filament rendering. d, Pinacidil-induced change in diameter (%) calculated at 10 μM and 100 μM perfusion of pinacidil over cranial windows for WT and SVD mice (n = 4 mice, with 3–5 ACT zone segments measured from each mouse). **P = 0.0082, *P = 0.0471 via two-sided ANOVA, post-hoc Šidák’s multiple-comparison test performed on mouse averages. e, Summary data of glibenclamide-induced change in diameter calculated at 10 μM and 100 μM perfusion of glibenclamide for WT and SVD mice (n = 4 mice, 3–5 ACT zone segments measured from each mouse). ****P < 0.0001, **P = 0.0035 via two-sided ANOVA, post-hoc Šidák’s multiple-comparison test performed on mouse averages. Illustration in a created in BioRender; Jeffrey, D. https://BioRender.com/9b4mcyy (2026).\n\nSource data",
