      "domain": "1",
      "title": "Pericyte KATP channel hyperactivity redistributes cortical blood flow in a CADASIL mouse model",
      "authors": "Danielle A. Jeffrey; Eric W. Prince; Niloufar Khakpour; Hannah R. Ferris; Colin H. Peters; Gregory Seedorf; Phinea Z. Romero; Mayra Bueno Guerrero; Abigail N. Russell; Katherine Glodoski; Gregory L. Futia; Stephanie K. Bonney; Michael A. Thornton; Emily A. Gibson; Catherine Proenza; Anastacia M. Garcia; Nikolaos M. Tsoukias; Fabrice Dabertrand",
      "figure": "Fig. 8",
      "caption": "Fig. 8\n\nPericyte KATP channel hyperactivity disrupts cortical perfusion homeostasis.\n\nIn vivo and ex vivo analyses reveal that ACT pericytes in SVD exhibit reduced ATP-related gene expression and are hyperpolarized under basal conditions due to constitutive KATP channel activity. This hyperactivity impairs pressure-induced constriction, disrupting pericyte-mediated regulation of local vascular resistance. As a result, the normal depth-dependent resistance gradient is lost, causing blood flow to be preferentially redirected along lower-resistance pathways to superficial cortical layers and reducing perfusion to deeper regions. Although total cortical perfusion is preserved, this redistribution leads to underperfusion of deeper territories, directly linking microvascular dysfunction to the selective vulnerability of deep brain tissue. Schematic created in BioRender; Jeffrey, D. https://BioRender.com/aao9pxo (2026).",
