      "figure": "Fig. 3.",
      "caption": "Fig. 3.\n\nPossible structures of cffDNA.\n\nApoptotic cells may release cffDNA in apoptotic EVs, which may become lysed to release their contents. Necrotic cells could generate long, irregular fragments of cffDNA. Necroptotic cells may release cffDNA with structural characteristics of apoptotic and/or necrotic cells. Alternatively, healthy and stressed cells may release cffDNA in large EVs via outward budding or in small EVs either by outward budding or as exosomes formed through endosomal maturation. Naked fetal DNA, including circular DNA and ultrashort DNA, alongside nonvesicular extracellular particles (e.g., DNA-protein-lipid complexes, termed virtosomes) associated with cffDNA could be released by cells under various physiological conditions, which are currently unknown. Created in BioRender. Sparkes, K. (2026); https://BioRender.com/b8fyd2b.",
