      "title": "A systematic comparison of single-cell perturbation response prediction models",
      "authors": "Lanxiang Li; Yue You; Yunlin Fu; Wenyu Liao; Xueying Fan; Shihong Lu; Ye Cao; Bo Li; Wenle Ren; Jiaming Kong; Shuangjia Zheng; Jizheng Chen; Xiaodong Liu; Luyi Tian",
      "figure": "Fig. 2.",
      "caption": "Fig. 2.\n\nPrediction of unseen single-gene perturbations across multiple models and datasets.\n\n(A) Schematic overview of the single-gene perturbation prediction task. Created in BioRender. L. Tian (2026), https://biorender.com/bjpu41h. (B) Absolute gene expression–level performance metrics. Pearson’s correlation coefficients on all genes and the top 20 genes across different datasets and models. (C) Cosine similarity (Papalexi dataset) and Pearson’s correlation coefficients (Sunshine dataset) on selected perturbation genes across models. The perturbation genes are ranked by impact strength. (D) Delta-based analysis of perturbation effect size and prediction accuracy predicted by the PerturbNet model on the Replogle dataset. Pearson correlation and delta direction accuracy were positively correlated (left), with larger-effect perturbations yielding higher delta direction accuracy (right). (E) Distribution comparison of the top five genes between predicted and observed expression changes on EGR4 perturbation of the Datlinger 2017 dataset. (F) Correlation of effect sizes between true and predicted perturbations relative to control. All statistics shown are computed using the subset of Task 1 test perturbations shared by all models. The median overlap accuracy of the top 100 DEGs across datasets is shown above, and the median DEG count per dataset is shown on the right. Models are ranked by overlap accuracy and datasets by median effect size. (G) Variance decomposition analysis showing the relative contribution of perturbation identity, model choice, and dataset identity to prediction performance across different evaluation metrics.",
