      "figure": "Figure 2",
      "caption": "Figure 2\n\nMicrobiome affecting airway disease. Colonization by 3 years of age of the nasopharynx with S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, and M. catarrhalis has been associated with the development of bronchiolitis and pneumonia by age 4 years. Specific microbiota in the gut has been associated with changes in airway function and asthma risk. Dysbiosis and reduced biodiversity of the gut microbiome can lead to increased susceptibility to respiratory viral infection due to modulation of metabolites and immune pathways through the gut-lung axis. Created in BioRender. Grayson, M. (2026) https://BioRender.com/v0oua63; License: CC-BY, Source: BioRender.",
