      "id": "9e931d0c",
      "diagram": "diagram/9e931d0c.png",
      "class": "Mechanism Diagram",
      "domain": "5",
      "title": "IFI16 restricts SARS-CoV-2 replication by disrupting nucleocapsid-driven phase separation",
      "authors": "Ilaria Cislaghi; Sarah Turati; Dalila Vicario; Gloria Griffante; Shikha Chandel; Irene Lo Cigno; Ranieri Bizzarri; Barbara Storti; Tina Ukmar Godec; Milan Zachrdla; Markus Zweckstetter; Lucia Signorini; Kevin Kamau Maina; Serena Delbue; Cinzia Borgogna; Marisa Gariglio",
      "figure": "Fig. 7",
      "caption": "Fig. 7\n\nSchematic representation of the viral replication cycle in IFI16-proficient (TC) or IFI16-deficient (IFI16KO) cells.\n\nThe SARS-CoV-2 spike protein binds to the host receptor angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2), promoting viral entry. Once inside the cell, the viral genome is released into the cytosol through uncoating. Viral RNA synthesis and transcription of subgenomic mRNAs occur within double-membrane vesicles (DMVs). The subgenomic mRNAs are then translated into the structural proteins of the virus. Among these, the N protein binds to the newly synthesized viral genomic RNA and undergoes LLPS, promoting viral genome packaging into ribonucleoprotein complexes. Simultaneously, the other viral structural proteins translocate into the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) membranes and pass through the ER-Golgi intermediate compartment (ERGIC), where interaction with N-encapsidated, newly produced genomic RNA results in budding into the lumen of the secretory vesicular compartment. Finally, virions are secreted from the infected cell through exocytosis. In IFI16-proficient cells (TC; left panel), after infection, IFI16 translocates from the nucleus to the cytoplasm, where it binds to the N protein and interferes with the LLPS phenomenon. Conversely, in IFI16-deficient cells (IFI16KO; right panel), the viral genome is efficiently packaged into ribonucleoprotein complexes. Due to the absence of IFI16 interference in N/vRNA LLPS, N condensates are larger, and IFI16-deficient cells release a higher number of virions compared to IFI16-proficient cells. Created with BioRender.com licensed under CC BY 4.0. https://BioRender.com/i79b753.",
      "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-026-10363-0",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
      "condition": "Specific BioRender page explicitly licenses this Completed Graphic under CC BY 4.0; individual BioRender icons may not be extracted and redistributed separately. Article permissions cover the saved author text under CC BY 4.0. Selected excerpts and captions contain no identified credit requiring separate permission. Other article images are omitted. Image bytes unchanged; complete BioRender public PNG, no cropping or icon extraction. Original-language article paragraphs and linked captions converted from JATS XML to Markdown; styling removed, no OCR, translation or paraphrase. Graphic: Figure 7. Schematic representation of the viral replication cycle in IFI16-proficient (TC) or IFI16-deficient (IFI16KO) cells.. Creator: Cinzia Borgogna. Provider: BioRender. Asset source: https://biorender.com/i79b753. License evidence: https://app.biorender.com/citation/6a058a18e205077b215af0d9. “Figure 7. Schematic representation of the viral replication cycle in IFI16-proficient (TC) or IFI16-deficient (IFI16KO) cells.” created in BioRender. Borgogna, C. (https://BioRender.com/i79b753) is licensed under CC BY 4.0. Article caption explicitly links to the complete BioRender asset; may correspond to a panel or construction sheet rather than the entire published figure.",
