      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1\n\nOverview of sex differences in fatty acid and triglyceride trafficking to the liver in the fasting and postprandial state.\n\nIn the (A) fasting state, women tend to have higher systemic NEFA concentrations than men, which may lead to a greater FA flux to the liver. In the transition to the (B) postprandial state, although it remains unclear if men and women, when fed an experimental test meal, achieve similar systemic NEFA concentrations, chylomicron-derived fatty acid spillover has been reported to be higher in women compared with men. Figure created in https://BioRender.com. Dennis, K. (2026) https://BioRender.com/0gws00e. Symbols and abbreviations: ♀ premenopausal women; ♂ men; ?, unclear due to divergent findings; =, no difference between sexes; AT, adipose tissue; NEFA, non‐esterified fatty acid; TG, triglyceride; CM, chylomicrons; FA, fatty acid.",
