      "figure": "Fig. 2.",
      "caption": "Fig. 2.\n\nThe effects of S-containing bacterial volatiles on plant growth, nutrition, defense, and physiological processes. From left to right: the first panel illustrates how S-containing bacterial volatiles—including dimethyl disulfide (DMDS), 2,4-dithiapentane (2,4-DTP), dimethyl trisulfide (DMTS), 3-(methylthio)-1-propanol (MTP), and 2-methyl-5-(methylthio)furan (2M5MTF)—promote growth in several plant species. The second panel shows that S-containing volatiles, particularly DMDS, can be directly utilized by plant metabolism as an environmental S source, leading to increased plant S nutrition. The third panel depicts the protective effects of S-containing volatiles—DMDS, DMTS, and S-methyl methanethiosulfonate (MMTS)—against various plant pathogens. The fourth panel highlights the priming functions of S-containing volatiles, which modulate multiple plant physiological processes, including increased plant biomass, alterations in S and nitrogen metabolism, changes in the composition of root-associated bacterial communities, and modifications of root exudate metabolite profiles. Created in BioRender. Türksoy, G.M. (2026) https://BioRender.com/u3v90b9.",
