      "domain": "5",
      "title": "Sulfur as a central integrator of plant–microbe interactions: from nutrient cycling to immune signalling and microbiome assembly",
      "authors": "Büsra Elkatmis; Gözde Merve Türksoy; Enrique Rodríguez; Bilal Rahmoune; Anna Koprivova; Stanislav Kopriva; Karl-Josef Dietz",
      "figure": "Fig. 5.",
      "caption": "Fig. 5.\n\nMechanisms employed by beneficial microbes to modulate S metabolism under abiotic stress. This figure summarizes some of the S-related mechanisms employed by beneficial microbes to mitigate abiotic stress-induced oxidative damage in plants. Panel 1. Alkaline stress increases soil pH, reduces nutrient availability, and enhances ROS accumulation. Bacillus sp. Jrh14-10 enhances the expression of S assimilation-related genes and antioxidant enzymes. Panel 2. Sulfur deficiency. Low S availability reduces amino acid biosynthesis and chlorophyll content. An 18-member SynCom secretes extracellular GSH, thereby alleviating the effects of S deficiency. Panel 3. Heavy metal stress impairs nutrient uptake and metabolic functions. Inoculation with three metal-resistant bacterial strains increases ascorbic acid (AsA) and GSH levels in Brassica napus grown in copper-contaminated soil, thereby reducing ROS-induced damage. Panel 4. Light stress reduces photosynthetic efficiency and chlorophyll content. Enterobacter sp. SA187 enhances redox homeostasis by modulating the GSH/glutaredoxin (GRX) balance and regulating the expression of photosynthesis-related genes, including LHCB (light-harvesting chlorophyll a/b-binding protein), PSAs (PSI subunits), and PSBs (PSII subunits). Panel 5. Salt stress induces ionic toxicity, leading to elevated ROS levels in plants. SA187 regulates ethylene signaling via 2-keto-4-methylthiobutyric acid (KMBA), activating the sulfur regulon and protecting plants from ROS-associated damage. Created in BioRender. Elkatmis, B. (2026) https://BioRender.com/s86ph1e.",
      "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.1093/jxb/erag186",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
