      "caption": "Figure 1\n\nGeneral principles of automated subject tracking in behavioral studies. (A) An exemplary open field-type arena with four mice inside, highlighted with their respective pose estimation keypoints and bounding boxes. Keypoints and bboxes are marked with colors here to differentiate between subjects. Red and blue mice are easy to distinguish in that configuration, as their bounding boxes are far apart. Green and yellow mice may pose difficulty in maintaining their stable identities, as their bounding boxes are very close to each other and overlap significantly. This is more problematic the longer they stay together. (B) With bounding box implementation (marked in yellow), identity of a subject is maintained by comparison of bboxes of the current frame (n) and immediate prior candidate frames (n-nc). Candidates (nc) can consist of only one frame or multiple frames, with the latter one having possibly higher chance of improving tracking efficiency. Exemplary features like euclidian distance between centroids of bboxes or their overlap can be used for comparison. The smallest the euclidian distance and the highest the overlap, the bigger the chance that adjacent bounding boxes belongs to the same subject. This is also a reason why animals that stay very close to each other for longer periods of time are prone to being mismatched. (C) Tracking with fiducial (ArUco) method involves attachment of a tag onto the subject, ensuring constant availability of the code for a designated camera to scan it (Coulombe et al., 2025). (D) Tracking with fiducial (ArUco) method involves a video framerate-dependent, high throughput code scanning for identity retrieval and a tag centroid placement. Continuous tracking of centroid position can serve as an identity reference and be used for potential correction of mismatched identities, delivered with AI tools. Potential correction can be applied by calculation of the exemplary euclidian distances between ArUco centroid and AI-derived corresponding keypoints of all subjects from the same current frame (n). The shortest distance should correspond to the same animal. If it is not, AI-derived identity can be overwritten with ArUco’s referenced identity. (E) Tracking with RFiD technology requires transponder placement in a body of the animal, usually subcutaneously to ensure minimal proximity to a reader. Areas around the neck and abdomen are commonly chosen. (F) An exemplary open field-type arena with circular, evenly spaced readers’ antennas (based on de Chaumont et al., 2019). Antennas have constant current flowing in them under the control of the board (for simplicity only one control board is presented, in reality each antenna has its own board and all of them may connect through USB-hub into the computer). If an animal equipped with RFiD tag reaches its range, the identity signal is transmitted and registered by the control board and computer software. (G) An exemplary open field-type arena with painted mice available for color tracking. For each color an appropriate threshold is applied in a separate layer (for simplicity only red color layer is shown). A centroid is then calculated and applied. Continuous tracking of centroid position can serve as an identity reference in a similar manner as with previous methods, with euclidian distance measurements being used to correct any potential identity mismatches. Created with BioRender.com (https://BioRender.com/got9sta).",
      "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fnmol.2026.1960212",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
      "condition": "Specific BioRender page explicitly licenses this Completed Graphic under CC BY 4.0; individual BioRender icons may not be extracted and redistributed separately. Article permissions cover the saved author text under CC BY 4.0. Selected excerpts and captions contain no identified credit requiring separate permission. Other article images are omitted. Image bytes unchanged; complete BioRender public PNG, no cropping or icon extraction. Original-language article paragraphs and linked captions converted from JATS XML to Markdown; styling removed, no OCR, translation or paraphrase. Graphic: General principles of automated subject tracking in behavioral studies. Creator: Bartosz Zglinicki. Provider: BioRender. Asset source: https://biorender.com/got9sta. License evidence: https://app.biorender.com/citation/6aa8fb1420ea6a6e8b6ada89. “General principles of automated subject tracking in behavioral studies” created in BioRender. Zglinicki, B. (https://BioRender.com/got9sta) is licensed under CC BY 4.0. Article caption explicitly links to the complete BioRender asset; may correspond to a panel or construction sheet rather than the entire published figure.",
