      "caption": "Fig. 4\n\nDetecting spatially variable isoforms using spatial autocorrelation.\n\na, Schematic example of a pattern (left) that can be detected using either predefined regions and spatial autocorrelation and a pattern (right) that can only be detected using spatial autocorrelation. b,c, Number (b) and percentage (c) of significant SVIs (FDR < 0.05) per dataset and cell type. Each bar represents a single proportion; error bars indicate 95% Wilson score confidence intervals. d, The overlap of significant isoforms across the datasets. Note, not all isoforms were tested in all datasets due to differences in read depth. e, Comparing the uncorrected P values obtained using normal and cell-type-constrained permutations for the isoforms significant in Sample 1 + 2 (AE) using all cells. f, Spatial distribution of Snap25-201 across the cortical layers in Sample 1 (AE). g, Mean relative expression of Snap25-201 in different cortical layers. Colors indicate the different datasets. Lower relative expression of Snap25-201 in layer 5 implies higher relative expression of Snap25-202. h, Number of significant isoforms per gene in Sample 1 + 2 (AE). i, Spatial distribution of the four significant isoforms of Arpp19 in Sample 1 (AE). j, Spatial distribution of Rps24-202 in oligodendrocytes of Sample 1 (AE). k, Overlap between the significant genes found using both methods for the excitatory neurons in Sample 1 + 2 (AE) data. l, Spatial distribution of Ighm-new in Sample 1 (AE), which was only discovered using spatial autocorrelation. f,i,j,l, Every hexagon shows the mean relative expression of the underlying cells (Methods). The schematic gene diagram shows the structures the most common isoforms in our data. ALL, all cells; EX, excitatory neurons; IN, inhibitory neurons; OLI, oligodendrocyte; AST, astrocyte; AE, AllExome dataset, 3.3 K = 3.3 K targeted dataset. Panel a created in BioRender; Jarroux, J. https://BioRender.com/8po0zmg (2026).",
