      "title": "The ZFP36 Family as Post-Transcriptional Regulators in Physiology and Disease",
      "authors": "Yuxuan Wen; Lichao Peng; Jing Wang",
      "figure": "Figure 4",
      "caption": "Figure 4\n\nPathological mechanisms of TTP dysregulation in chronic inflammatory and autoimmune diseases. TTP functional inactivation relieves post-transcriptional repression of its pro-inflammatory targets, driving multiple autoimmune diseases. (A) Rheumatoid arthritis (RA): Silenced TTP derepresses TNF-α, IL-6 and COX-2, promoting joint destruction; accumulated CCL3 and CXCL1/2 drive synovial neutrophil infiltration and further joint erosion. (B) Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD): TTP functional loss elevates IL-23α and IL-17α, activating the pathogenic IL-23/IL-17 axis; upregulated NLRP3 promotes inflammasome assembly, collectively driving mucosal damage. (C) Psoriasis: TTP downregulation raises IL-6 and CXCL8 and, together with NLRP3 inflammasome activation, drives epidermal hyperplasia and psoriatic lesions. Throughout, the elevated but functionally silenced state of TTP typical of chronic disease is distinguished from simple loss of expression. Solid arrows: Direct molecular regulation; dashed arrows: downstream pathological consequences. Red upward/downward arrows indicate upregulation/downregulation, respectively. Created in BioRender. koso, T. (2026) https://BioRender.com/tcagygb.",
      "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27146378",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
