      "id": "679af0e4",
      "diagram": "diagram/679af0e4.png",
      "class": "Mechanism Diagram",
      "domain": "5",
      "title": "Decoding a polyamine signal to direct vascular fate: thermospermine meets the ribosome",
      "authors": "Lázara Aline Simões Silva; Munevver Dogramaci; Wagner Campos Otoni",
      "figure": "Fig. 1.",
      "caption": "Fig. 1.\n\nConverging genetic and molecular evidence positions ribosome composition and RNA processing as prerequisite gatekeepers of thermospermine responsiveness. Thermospermine (tSpm), synthesized by ACL5 in vascular precursor cells, is decoded by OVAC-methylated ribosomes carrying the m3U2952 modification at the peptidyl transferase center. Two independent lines of evidence reinforce this ribosome-centric model. First, suppressor alleles of RPL10A and RPL4A (Mutsuda et al., 2025) selectively enhance translation of thermospermine-responsive uORF-containing transcripts without broadly increasing translational output, indicating that ribosome composition modulates access to defined mRNA subsets. Second, thermospermine-insensitive mutants (its1–its4; Saraumi et al., 2025), which accumulate normal levels of thermospermine yet fail to execute canonical responses, map to RNA processing and modification factors—including a SPOUT-domain methyltransferase (ITS1, identical to OVAC), the rRNA pseudouridine synthase CBF5/NAP57, the spliceosome disassembly factor STIPL1/NTR1, and the RNA-binding protein PHIP1—establishing that a permissive RNA and ribosome environment is required upstream of signal interpretation. On OVAC-modified ribosomes, thermospermine exerts a bifunctional translational output (Ko et al., 2026): it simultaneously up-regulates SACL family transcripts (via uORF derepression) and down-regulates LHW translation. SACL proteins compete with TMO5 for LHW binding, and reduced LHW availability further dampens the pro-differentiation TMO5–LHW heterodimer, ultimately suppressing xylem differentiation and limiting vascular proliferation. Created in BioRender. Silva, L.A. S. (2026) https://BioRender.com/qihsb6i.",
      "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.1093/jxb/erag257",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
      "condition": "Specific BioRender page explicitly licenses this Completed Graphic under CC BY 4.0; individual BioRender icons may not be extracted and redistributed separately. Article permissions cover the saved author text under CC BY 4.0. Selected excerpts and captions contain no identified credit requiring separate permission. Other article images are omitted. Image bytes unchanged; complete BioRender public PNG, no cropping or icon extraction. Original-language article paragraphs and linked captions converted from JATS XML to Markdown; styling removed, no OCR, translation or paraphrase. Graphic: Converging genetic and molecular evidence positions ribosome composition and RNA processing as prerequisite gatekeepers of thermospermine responsiveness.. Creator: Lázara aline Simões. Provider: BioRender. Asset source: https://biorender.com/qihsb6i. License evidence: https://app.biorender.com/citation/6a0f0ae401c8915e1c4d8048. “Converging genetic and molecular evidence positions ribosome composition and RNA processing as prerequisite gatekeepers of thermospermine responsiveness.” created in BioRender. Simões, L. A. (https://BioRender.com/qihsb6i) is licensed under CC BY 4.0. Article caption explicitly links to the complete BioRender asset; may correspond to a panel or construction sheet rather than the entire published figure.",
