      "authors": "Shouyao Zhang; Xiaochun Yang; Chenggui Xu; Shuang Wang; Xiantao Tai",
      "figure": "FIGURE 1",
      "caption": "FIGURE 1\n\nThe role of mitochondria in scleral fibroblasts. Type I collagen, synthesized by scleral fibroblasts, is the primary structural protein responsible for maintaining the morphology of the sclera. The energy provided by mitochondria sustains the active metabolic state of scleral fibroblasts, enabling them to secrete a substantial amount of collagen and maintain the biomechanical properties of the sclera. The relative hypoxia in the sclera alters the microenvironment of scleral fibroblasts, leading to mitochondrial dysfunction. This, in turn, accelerates the degradation of the extracellular matrix, ultimately contributing to the development of myopia. Created in BioRender. Xu, C (2026) https://BioRender.com/3eccrwy. Publication license obtained. RER:Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum; ATP: Adenosine Triphosphate; ECM: Extracellular Matrix, α-KG: α-Ketoglutarate; TGF:Transforming Growth Factor; MMP:Matrix Metalloproteinase; NAT: N-acetyltransferase; HIF1:Hypoxia-inducible factor 1.",
