      "authors": "Jonas Rotter; Toni Kirmann; Jana Brendler; Larissa Anthofer; Nina Maria Wilpert; Stefan Hallermann; Heike Biebermann; Ingo Bechmann",
      "figure": "Fig. 9",
      "caption": "Fig. 9\n\nPerspectives on MCT8 expression in the human brain. a MCT8 is expressed across multiple brain regions, including the hippocampus, hypothalamus, and both cortical and subcortical areas. The regional distribution and clinical findings in MCT8-deficient patients indicate that sufficient MCT8 expression is essential for diverse neurological functions. Its widespread presence in the brain tissue suggests ongoing physiological relevance for these functions beyond neurodevelopment. b At the cellular level, MCT8 mediates TH transport between various cell types – such as endothelial cells, perivascular cells, astrocytes, and neurons – while being notably absent from microglia. c Subcellularly, MCT8 localizes to the plasma membrane and intracellular compartments, particularly near or at the nuclear lamina, suggesting roles in membrane-bound, intracellular, and possibly nuclear TH transport, potentially via endosomal pathways. Created in BioRender. Rotter, J. (2026) (https://BioRender.com/s08esrp, https://BioRender.com/qa325ul and https://BioRender.com/2o10zh3)",
