      "id": "033c25f9",
      "diagram": "diagram/033c25f9.png",
      "class": "Mechanism Diagram",
      "domain": "1",
      "title": "NLRP3-mediated pyroptosis in cardiovascular disease: from molecular mechanisms to therapeutic targets",
      "authors": "Qiuyu Xu; Wei Sun; Linwei Li; Ning Liu; Bin Liu",
      "figure": "FIGURE 2",
      "caption": "FIGURE 2\n\nSchematic diagram illustrating the mechanism of pyroptosis. This figure illustrates the classical and non-classical signaling pathways of pyroptosis. In the classical pathway, pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) or damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) activate pattern recognition receptors (such as TLR4/NF-κB), thereby upregulating the transcriptional expression of the NLRP3 inflammasome, pro-IL-1β, and pro-IL-18. Subsequently, ASC and pro-caspase-1 are recruited; activated caspase-1 cleaves GSDMD to generate the pore-forming GSDMD-N fragment, inducing cell membrane perforation and the release of cellular contents, and also cleaves pro-IL-1β and pro-IL-18 into their mature forms, IL-1β and IL-18, thereby exacerbating the inflammatory response. In the non-classical pathway, intracellular LPS directly activates caspase-4/5 (mouse caspase-11), which in turn cleaves GSDMD and indirectly activates the NLRP3 inflammasome, amplifying inflammatory signaling. Under specific stimuli, activated caspase-3 cleaves GSDME, releasing the GSDME-NT porogenic fragment, which induces pyroptosis. The death of mouse macrophages induced by Yersinia YopJ and other factors can be mediated by caspase-8-induced cleavage of gasdermin D. As a new mechanism of immune killing, NK cells can deliver granzyme A into target cells via perforin. GZMA directly cleaves the GSDMB protein within target cells, releasing its N-terminal pore-forming fragment. The excessive activation of this pathway mediates cardiomyocyte pyroptosis, vascular endothelial dysfunction, and immune cell infiltration, which constitute important pathological foundations for cardiovascular diseases such as atherosclerosis, myocardial ischemia/reperfusion injury, and heart failure. Xu, Q. (2026). Schematic diagram illustrating the mechanism of pyroptosis. Created in BioRender. https://BioRender.com/7h4e1fd.",
      "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2026.1901368",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
      "condition": "Specific BioRender page explicitly licenses this Completed Graphic under CC BY 4.0; individual BioRender icons may not be extracted and redistributed separately. Article permissions cover the saved author text under CC BY 4.0. Selected excerpts and captions contain no identified credit requiring separate permission. Other article images are omitted. Image bytes unchanged; complete BioRender public PNG, no cropping or icon extraction. Original-language article paragraphs and linked captions converted from JATS XML to Markdown; styling removed, no OCR, translation or paraphrase. Graphic: Schematic diagram illustrating the mechanism of pyroptosis. Creator: Qiuyu Xu. Provider: BioRender. Asset source: https://biorender.com/7h4e1fd. License evidence: https://app.biorender.com/citation/6a2ac760916e53e7937e3bda. “Schematic diagram illustrating the mechanism of pyroptosis” created in BioRender. Xu, Q. (https://BioRender.com/7h4e1fd) is licensed under CC BY 4.0. Article caption explicitly links to the complete BioRender asset; may correspond to a panel or construction sheet rather than the entire published figure.",
