      "figure": "Figure 5",
      "caption": "Figure 5\n\nWorkflow of iTP-seq for high-throughput analysis of antibiotic-induced ribosome stalling. A biotinylated and polyadenylated mRNA library with a randomized coding region is translated in vitro, followed by RNase R digestion to generate ribosome-protected fragments (inverse toeprints). The growing peptide is represented as a chain of beads, antibiotic–by a star. After affinity purification and linker ligation, inverse toeprints are reverse-transcribed, PCR-amplified, and size-selected by PAGE to isolate fragments corresponding to ribosome stalling within the randomized region (indicated as a major band on the gel within a red rectangle). Sequenced fragments are then mapped to the mRNA library, allowing identification of ribosome arrest motifs with codon-level resolution. Created in BioRender. Sergiev, P. (2026) https://BioRender.com/muwq0zn (accessed on 11 June 2026).",
