      "id": "54bd581c",
      "diagram": "diagram/54bd581c.png",
      "class": "Mechanism Diagram",
      "domain": "5",
      "title": "The Impact of Peptidoglycan Structure on Immune Sensing",
      "authors": "Sasha Cardozo; Ciaran Skerry",
      "figure": "Figure 3",
      "caption": "Figure 3\n\nDefects in PGN recycling drive the release of structurally diverse fragments. N. gonorrhoeae carries C-terminal point mutations in AmpG that impair its transport function (left), and B. pertussis harbors an IS481 insertion that reduces AmpG expression (center); these defects impair muropeptide reuptake, driving the constitutive release of >80% and >95% 1,6-anhydro muramyl tri-/tetrapeptide (TCT), respectively. B. burgdorferi lacks AmpG and the recycling machinery entirely and incorporates host-derived L-ornithine and tick-derived GlcNAc into its PGN, releasing >45% ornithine-containing muropeptides (right). Across all three pathogens, defective recycling drives extracellular accumulation of immunostimulatory PGN fragments that have unique downstream signaling implications. [Created in BioRender. Cardozo, S. (2026) https://BioRender.com/d8iu2mw].",
      "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.3390/toxins18080354",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
      "condition": "Specific BioRender page explicitly licenses this Completed Graphic under CC BY 4.0; individual BioRender icons may not be extracted and redistributed separately. Article permissions cover the saved author text under CC BY 4.0. Selected excerpts and captions contain no identified credit requiring separate permission. Other article images are omitted. Image bytes unchanged; complete BioRender public PNG, no cropping or icon extraction. Original-language article paragraphs and linked captions converted from JATS XML to Markdown; styling removed, no OCR, translation or paraphrase. Graphic: Defects in PGN recycling drive the release of structurally diverse frag-ments. Creator: Sasha Cardozo. Provider: BioRender. Asset source: https://biorender.com/d8iu2mw. License evidence: https://app.biorender.com/citation/6a85cafb6f12c4587010a077. “Defects in PGN recycling drive the release of structurally diverse frag-ments” created in BioRender. Cardozo, S. (https://BioRender.com/d8iu2mw) is licensed under CC BY 4.0. Article caption explicitly links to the complete BioRender asset; may correspond to a panel or construction sheet rather than the entire published figure.",
