      "diagram": "diagram/3a2f9313.png",
      "class": "Mechanism Diagram",
      "domain": "5",
      "title": "Active transcriptome remodeling underlies antimony-induced drug tolerance in persister-like Leishmania parasites",
      "authors": "Eyson Quiceno; Cristian Camilo Rodríguez-Almonacid; Khalid Omeir; Jacob Ancira; Caleb Phillips; Zemfira N Karamysheva; Hira L Nakhasi",
      "figure": "Fig 8",
      "caption": "Fig 8\n\nSchematic representation of biological processes altered during formation of persister-like Leishmania parasites.\n\nFollowing a drug challenge, surviving persister-like parasites remodel their transcription through a general downregulation of structural and metabolic pathways, entering a dormant-like state. Upon drug removal, recovered parasites upregulate macromolecular biogenesis, alongside energetic and metabolic processes, to exit dormancy and resume proliferation. When subjected to a second drug challenge, rechallenged persister-like parasites repress drug uptake, metabolism, and protein synthesis while activating drug efflux leading to enhanced drug tolerance. This dual mechanism results in a rapid, primed response, efficient metabolic quiescence, and enhanced survival under recurrent drug pressure. Created in BioRender. Rodriguez, C. (2026) https://BioRender.com/dl3imnc.",
      "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.ppat.1014682",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
