      "title": "Androgen and glucocorticoid crosstalk in granulosa cells: molecular insights into polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome",
      "authors": "Martina Pinna; Alessandra Tomaselli; Davide Ferrari; Marianna Minnetti; Daniele Gianfrilli; Elisa Giannetta; Carlotta Pozza; Andrea M Isidori; Federica Barbagallo; Riccardo Pofi",
      "figure": "Figure 2",
      "caption": "Figure 2\n\nDysregulated HPO feedback and proposed model of granulosa cell dysfunction in PMOS/PCOS. (A) Schematic representation of the dysregulated HPO axis in PMOS. Increased pulsatile secretion of GnRH from the hypothalamus promotes preferential release of LH over FSH by the anterior pituitary. Elevated LH stimulates androgen production by ovarian theca cells, leading to hyperandrogenism, impaired follicular maturation, anovulation, and the accumulation of immature follicles characteristic of polycystic ovaries. (B) Proposed mechanisms linking AR and GR signaling to granulosa cell dysfunction. Excess androgens and maladaptive glucocorticoid signaling converge on deregulation of AR and GR respectively, resulting in transcriptional imbalance through interactions at AREs and GREs. The +/− sign indicates context-dependent AR effects, whereas the - sign denotes the predominantly detrimental effects proposed for sustained or maladaptive GR activation. This process may be further amplified by chronic stress, insulin resistance/hyperinsulinemia, and altered local cortisol metabolism mediated by 11β-HSD1/2. The resulting dysregulation of FSHR, CYP19A1, and StAR, together with mitochondrial dysfunction, increased ROS production, apoptosis, and reduced oocyte support, contributes to persistent androgen excess, granulosa cell dysfunction, follicular arrest, and chronic anovulation. PMOS, Polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome; PCOS, polycystic ovary syndrome; HPO, hypothalamic–pituitary–ovarian; GnRH, gonadotropin-releasing hormone; LH, luteinizing hormone; FSH, follicle-stimulating hormone; AR, androgen receptor; GR, glucocorticoid receptor; ARE, androgen response element; GRE, glucocorticoid response element; 11β-HSD1/2, 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1 and type 2; FSHR, follicle-stimulating hormone receptor; CYP19A1, cytochrome P450 family 19 subfamily A member 1 (aromatase); StAR, steroidogenic acute regulatory protein; ROS, reactive oxygen species. Created in BioRender. Pinna, M (2026). https://BioRender.com/gefpgkm.",
