      "title": "Data-free Neural Representation Compression with Riemannian Neural Dynamics",
      "authors": "Zhengqi Pei; Anran Zhang; Shuhui Wang; Xiangyang Ji; Qingming Huang",
      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1: Computing a Neural Riemannian Metric: We interpret an arbitrary weight matrix W e Rm x n of a neural model as the dynamical interactions between m input neurons and n output neurons. Each neuron is interpreted as Dq-dimensional dynamical state, i.e., qin), qbout) e IRDa, with α E [1, m], y E [1, n]. We assume Dq = 3 in this figure. Following this, we interpret each weight value Wry as the scalar-based relation measured by a Riemannian metric g : RDa × R Da -→ R such that W ry = g(qin), q(out). The exact analytical form of this Riemannian metric is as follows. The dimensional components of qin) and qbout) are mutually operated to generate Dg displacement vectors, i.e., d(s) e RDa, s E [1, Da]. Then, we merge these displacement vectors via a non-linear summation operation, followed by a R Da → R projection that results in the scalar-based relation, which is expected to approximate each weight value.",
