      "id": "aeddf2e7",
      "diagram": "diagram/aeddf2e7.jpg",
      "class": "Mechanism Diagram",
      "domain": "0",
      "title": "How Interpretable Are Interpretable Graph Neural Networks?",
      "authors": "Yongqiang Chen; Yatao Bian; Bo Han; James Cheng",
      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1. Illustration of Subgraph Multilinear Extension approximation failure. In a binary graph classification task, XGNNs need to classify whether a graph contains a specific “house\"' or “cycle\"' motif into two steps: (a) Subgraph Extraction, a subgraph extractor computes the sampling probability for each edge using the attention mechanism, which further determines the subgraph distribution: part of the “house\"' or “grid\"' motif is sampled with a certain probability. The expected subgraph Ge to be sampled according to the subgraph distribution is a “soft\"' subgraph, with each edge weighting the corresponding sampling probability. (b) Subgraph Classification, each subgraph G corresponds to a respective label distribution P(Y |Ge) (e.g., when some key parts of the house\" motif are sampled, the “house\" label probability will be higher). The final label predictions are conditioned on the subgraph distributions, averaged upon each P(Y |G) with the probability of G being sampled. The averaged label distribution leads to a prediction of “house\" for the example. Instead of averaging the subgraph conditional label predictions, previous methods directly take the expected “soft\"' subgraph as the input to the subgraph classifier GNN fc, which can be biased and lead to an incorrect prediction of cycle\".",
      "sourceUrl": "https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/chen24b.html",
