      "title": "Are VLMs Seeing or Just Saying? Uncovering the Illusion of Visual Re-examination",
      "authors": "Shi, Chufan; Yang, Cheng; Wu, Yaokang; Jin, Linghao; Shui, Bo; Berg-Kirkpatrick, Taylor; Ma, Xuezhe",
      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1. Illustrative example of visual re-examination. Left: Given image Ia and query Q, the VLM generates reasoning chain Ra including a self-reflective trigger. Top-right: Under standard inference with Ia, the model validates its logic to reach the correct answer A1. Bottom-right: In the VIsUALS wAP condition, Ia is replaced by a visually similar but semantically distinct image Ib post-reflection. Despite explicitly “saying\" it will re-examine the figure, the model fails to detect the visual discrepancy and yields an incorrect answer A2 grounded in the stale context of Ia. Only an ideal model exhibits genuine visual grounding to reach A3.",
