      "title": "Learning to Theorize the World from Observation",
      "authors": "Doojin Baek; Gyubin Lee; Junyeob Baek; Hosung Lee; Sungjin Ahn",
      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1. Learning to Theorize (L2T) Framework. (a) Training data consists of observation pairs (α, y) generated by unobserved true programs. (b) Under L2T, the model learns to discover reusable primitives (Rotate, Left, Down, and Paint) and to compose them into executable theories. (c) Without L2T, the model instead memorizes entangled composite primitives (e.g., Left-Down) as indecomposed single units. (d) Once the model has learned to theorize, novel phenomena (e.g., Down-Paint-Rotate) can be explained by recombining learned primitives. (e) In contrast, memorized entangled representations fail to generalize to unseen programs.",
