      "title": "Beyond Theorem Proving: Formulation, Framework and Benchmark for Formal Problem-Solving",
      "authors": "Qi Liu; Xinhao Zheng; Renqiu Xia; Xingzhi Qi; Qinxiang Cao; Junchi Yan",
      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1. Overview of reasoning paradigms. (a) Informal Reasoning relies on LLM generation. While flexible, it suffers from hallucinations and relies on (d) brittle ad-hoc answer checking. (b) Formal Theorem Proving (FTP) is limited to verifying propositions but can be combined with informal reasoning for post-hoc verification. Using factored formalization, the answer is externally derived and hardcoded. The formal system only validates the result, leaving the derivation process unverified. (c) Formal Problem-Solving (FPS) (Ours) unifies derivation and verification by constructing both the answer term and its proof within the formal environment. (e) Restricted Propositional Equivalence (RPE) leverages the formal kernel to verify semantic correctness for arbitrary mathematical structures.",
