      "authors": "David Huang; Jaewon Chang; Avidan Shah; Prateek Mittal; Chawin Sitawarin",
      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1. Overview of Rapid Response (RR) and poisoning attack vectors. The clean pipeline (top row) collects in-the-wild jailbreaks as references, proliferates them into synthetic training samples, and fine-tunes a guard model that correctly classifies these new jailbreak strategies as unsafe. Our poisoning attacks can be used for two practical objectives (two bottom rows): (i) Utility degradation: we poison the model to misclassify a subpopulation of safe queries (here, queries with the word “ChatGPT\") as unsafe. (i) Safety degradation: we introduce a more sophisticated Omission Attack that creates a backdoor which can evade detection when added to unsafe inputs. This will beexplained indetail inSection3.3andFigure3.",
