      "domain": "0",
      "title": "ResponseRank: Data-Efficient Reward Modeling through Preference Strength Learning",
      "authors": "Kaufmann, Timo; Metz, Yannick; Keim, Daniel; Hüllermeier, Eyke",
      "figure": "Figure 1",
      "caption": "Figure 1: ResponseRank learns strength-aware preferences from rankings over comparisons derived from implicit strength signals. We start from a dataset of comparisons, consisting of a pair of objects (ai, bi), a preference (pi E [a, b)), a strength signal (Ti, here scalar response times), and metadata (mi). (1) Comparisons are stratified using metadata mi (e.g., by annotator ID), ensuring local validity of the signal-strength relationship. The metadata can be discarded after this step. (2) Within each stratum, instances (ai, bi, Pi, Ti) are normalized to (wi, li, Ti) format based on the preference label pi, where w; is the preferred item and l; is the dispreferred item. (3) Rank construction then sorts these normalized tuples (e.g., by ascending response time Ti) and appends a virtual anchor element with a fixed score of O to create target rankings. (4) A preference predictor models these target rankings by predicting signed utility differences se(wi, li) = ue (wi) -- ue(li). (5) The se values (and the anchor's O) act as latent strengths for a Plackett-Luce model; NLL loss minimization yields parameters of the utility function (or reward model) ue.",
